WEBVTT

00:00:00.633 --> 00:00:13.327
Connect GitHub, and Worklynq can work with your code: tickets show their branches, commits and pull requests, and AI coding agents can work in your repositories. It’s the first step for software teams.

00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:19.669
In the Admin console, open Apps, and under Developer tools, click Connect on GitHub.

00:00:20.133 --> 00:00:29.136
Installing the Worklynq GitHub App is the recommended way. Click Continue to GitHub, and pick the account or organization that owns your code.

00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:39.584
Then choose the repositories Worklynq may use, and only those. Approve read and write access to their contents and pull requests, and you come back to Worklynq.

00:00:40.567 --> 00:00:46.839
GitHub now shows as connected, with your organization and how many repositories Worklynq can see.

00:00:48.300 --> 00:00:55.553
To add or remove repositories later, use Repos on GitHub. You can connect more than one organization, too.

00:00:56.000 --> 00:01:05.323
Then turn the GitHub section on for the ticket types that should show code, in Work Configuration, Ticket Types, Sections. It starts off for every type.

00:01:06.300 --> 00:01:16.241
Here’s a bug on a client’s patient app: large text breaks the appointment card. Each project is mapped to its repositories, so the ticket knows where its code lives.

00:01:16.700 --> 00:01:24.380
Its GitHub section shows every branch, commit and pull request that mentions the ticket’s key, with their state, straight from GitHub.

00:01:24.833 --> 00:01:30.977
Starting the work yourself? Copy the suggested branch name, and anything you push shows up here on its own.

